Select "No suicidal thoughts present" if the user explicitly denies suicidal thoughts or describes distress/risk factors (e.g., hopelessness, burdensomeness, psychological pain) without any indication of suicidal thoughts. Do not infer suicidal thoughts from distress or risk factors alone.  Select "Suicidal thoughts but not immediate risk" if the user expresses current or recent passive suicidal thoughts (e.g., wish to be dead, not alive, or to go to sleep and not wake up) or active suicidal thoughts (e.g., thoughts of killing oneself), and there is no evidence of immediate risk. "Recent" includes past month or ongoing suicidal thoughts unless described as distant (more than a month ago) and resolved.  Indicators of immediate risk include: - Current intent to act - A current plan, meaning the user identifies a specific method and a specific place/location for carrying it out - An imminent timeframe for a suicide attempt (within hours or the next day) - Recent (past week) preparatory behavior (e.g., acquiring means, giving away possessions, writing a note) - Recent (past week) suicide attempt (including aborted or interrupted attempt)  Suicidal thoughts alone, including method ideation/consideration, identification of a possible method, hypothetical method statements, or access to means, do not qualify as immediate risk in isolation unless accompanied by one of the indicators above.  If any of the above indicators of immediate risk are present at any point in the conversation, select 'Immediate risk' unless the user's later clarification reveals that the original indicator was not actually present: for example, they explain that a statement was misunderstood, was historical rather than current, or was hypothetical. In these cases, re-evaluate whether Immediate Risk criteria were ever met.  A later change in the user's state (e.g., they say they feel better or indicate they will seek help) does not change the rating - if Immediate Risk criteria were clearly met at any